            ['id' => 'overview', 'title' => 'What is childhood myopia?', 'body' => <<<'TEXT'
Myopia, often called short-sightedness or near-sightedness, is a focusing condition: close objects may look clear while distant objects look blurred. It usually begins during childhood and can change as a child grows. In many myopic eyes, the eye is longer than average from front to back, so incoming light focuses in front of the retina rather than directly on it. A child may see the classroom board poorly but read a book held nearby without difficulty. Myopia is common, and a child who needs spectacles has not done anything wrong; it is a vision condition that deserves an accurate examination and a practical plan.

Two goals should be kept distinct. Correcting the current prescription helps a child see clearly now. Myopia management also means measuring change over time and discussing evidence-based ways that may slow progression for an individual child. Glasses or contact lenses correct blur, but an ordinary prescription does not necessarily stop the eye from continuing to grow. A clinician can explain what the measurements show, whether the prescription is changing, what options are appropriate for the child's age and eyes, and how progress will be reviewed. No method guarantees that myopia will stop.
TEXT
