Ask the child what they can and cannot see in specific situations rather than only asking whether their vision is “fine.” Note when the difficulty began, whether it is getting more noticeable, and whether one eye seems different from the other. A family history of myopia is useful to mention, but it cannot diagnose a child. Sudden loss of vision, a painful red eye, new flashes or a curtain-like shadow are not routine signs of childhood myopia and need prompt medical attention.
TEXT
