A cataract is clouding of the eye’s normally clear natural lens, which helps focus light. It usually develops gradually, and age is a common factor, but cataracts can also occur in other circumstances, including after an eye injury or alongside certain health conditions. A cataract is not a film that can be washed away with drops. In its early stages it may cause little difficulty; when it changes vision, a person may notice that everyday tasks are becoming harder.

The decision to consider cataract surgery is based on how the cataract affects the person and what the examination shows—not simply on age or a particular visual-acuity number. New glasses, brighter lighting, or other adaptations may help some people for a time. When a cataract significantly interferes with daily life and surgery is appropriate, the cloudy natural lens is removed and an artificial intraocular lens (IOL) is placed. The correct choice depends on the whole eye and the person’s priorities, so no lens type is “best” for everyone.
TEXT
