The retina is the light-sensitive tissue lining the back of the eye. It converts light into signals that travel to the brain. The macula is its central area and supports detailed central vision used for reading and recognizing faces. The uvea is a group of structures that includes the iris, ciliary body and choroid. Problems in these tissues can affect vision in different ways, so “retina and uvea” describes a broad area rather than one disease or one treatment.

Retinal conditions may be related to diabetes, blood-vessel changes, age-related macular disease, a retinal tear or detachment, inflammation, injury or other causes. Uveitis means inflammation inside the eye; it can have an infectious or non-infectious cause and may be associated with other health conditions. The same symptom can have several explanations. An examination is necessary to identify the problem, understand whether it is urgent, and decide if additional testing or referral is needed.
TEXT
