The aim of a macular assessment is to understand what is causing the visual change, how the central retina is affected, and whether monitoring or treatment is appropriate. Some macular changes are slow; others need prompt attention. A diagnosis should be explained in plain language, including what is known, what remains uncertain, and what the next review is intended to detect. Information about one person’s injection, supplement or scan schedule should not be copied as advice for someone else.
