The cornea is the transparent front surface of the eye. It helps focus incoming light and protects the structures inside the eye. Its smoothness and clarity matter: swelling, scarring, infection, injury, dryness or an irregular shape can make vision blurred and the eye uncomfortable. Corneal care covers several different conditions, so the first step is to find the cause rather than treating every red or painful eye in the same way.

Keratoconus is a condition in which the cornea becomes thinner and changes shape, which can produce irregular astigmatism and blurred vision. Infection, abrasion, chemical injury, inherited disorders and contact-lens-related problems are separate possibilities. Some problems are minor, while others threaten sight and need urgent treatment. Avoid home remedies, old drops or another person’s contact lenses. An examination allows a clinician to check the surface, measure shape where appropriate, and decide what care is safe.
