            ['id' => 'overview', 'title' => 'What strabismus (squint) means', 'body' => <<<'TEXT'
Strabismus, commonly called a squint or misaligned eyes, means the eyes do not point in the same direction at the same time. One eye may turn inward, outward, upward or downward, and the direction can vary. It affects children and adults. A turn may be constant or appear only occasionally, particularly when a person is tired or unwell. A visible eye turn is a reason for assessment, but its appearance alone does not show how well each eye sees or what caused it.

When a child’s eyes do not work together, the brain may rely more on one eye and vision in the other may not develop normally; this can lead to amblyopia. Adults can also have double vision or a new misalignment for a range of reasons. Prompt assessment helps determine whether the concern is longstanding, new, or linked to another eye or health problem. Treatment aims vary: improving alignment, supporting visual development, reducing double vision or addressing an underlying cause.
TEXT
