Ask what outcome the proposed treatment is intended to improve, how success will be measured, what alternatives exist, whether alignment may change again, and what follow-up is needed. Some plans aim at comfortable single vision, others at visual development or alignment; expectations should be explained clearly. Surgery has risks and may require further treatment. The treatment chosen for a child should be explained to the caregiver and, in age-appropriate language, to the child as well. No specific approach suits every squint.
