Children can experience refractive errors, amblyopia, strabismus, cataract, eye-surface problems, injury and other conditions. Some are obvious; others are not. An early, suitable assessment is especially important when a caregiver, teacher or clinician notices a concern, when the child was born prematurely, or when there is a relevant family history. The purpose is not to medicalise normal childhood, but to find a problem that may benefit from timely care and explain what families can do next.
