Discuss discomfort, infection risk, medication interactions, transport and help at home. Confirm whether you should continue routine medicines and drops; never stop a medicine used for diabetes, blood pressure or immune health without the clinician who prescribed it. If you need an interpreter or an accessible written schedule, ask before the appointment. Keep procedure instructions with your medicine list and the phone number for reporting a problem. After treatment, follow the instructions for that specific procedure, not advice for someone else’s injection or laser.

Uveitis may prompt questions about general health because eye inflammation can sometimes be related to infection or a condition elsewhere in the body. This does not mean that every person needs broad testing. Your clinician selects investigations based on the pattern, history and examination. Tell the eye team about fever, skin changes, joint symptoms, recent infections and previous diagnoses when relevant; share results from other doctors if available. Ask who will explain test results and whether you should continue care with a general physician or another specialist.

Ask for a clear division of responsibilities when more than one team is involved: who prescribes each medicine, who monitors side effects, who orders imaging and whom to call if vision changes. This is especially helpful when a person sees different services for diabetes and retinal care. If the plan feels confusing, ask for one concise written summary. Clear coordination helps patients attend the right review at the right time and reduces the risk of stopping treatment or repeating a medicine by mistake.
TEXT
