        'body' => <<<'TEXT'
The word “macular” describes a location, not a single diagnosis. Ask the clinician to name the condition, whether it is active, which eye is affected, and whether a treatment is intended to improve current vision or reduce the chance of further change. A stable scan may support observation in one situation and still require repeat checks in another. If treatment is recommended, ask how the team will judge whether it is working and what finding would lead to a different approach.

If injections are discussed, ask what medicine is proposed, how it is given, the likely visit pattern, the main risks and the symptoms that need a same-day call afterwards. Ask whether observation or another option is reasonable and what could happen if treatment is delayed. Do not assume that another patient’s injection schedule applies to you; age, diagnosis, scan findings and response differ. Keep every appointment or call the clinic early if transport, cost or caregiving makes attendance difficult, so the care team can advise safely.

Some people hear about vitamins or supplements after an AMD diagnosis. Research evidence applies to certain categories and stages; it does not mean every person should take the same product. Tell the ophthalmologist about all vitamins and medicines before starting a supplement, particularly if you smoke or have other health conditions. Ask whether the evidence applies to your findings, what amount is appropriate if one is recommended, and whether the product could interact with another treatment. Food and supplements should not be used to delay evaluation of new distortion.

Keep a dated record of visual changes and test results. If a new distortion appears between appointments, note when it began and contact the care team rather than waiting to document it at the next visit. Home grids can help describe change but are not a clearance test. Encourage family members to ask before taking over tasks; many people with reduced central vision remain capable of independent decisions and benefit most from practical tools that preserve control over daily life.
