Corneal conditions can change how glasses and contact lenses work. If a prescription changes repeatedly or vision remains distorted despite updated spectacles, ask whether the corneal shape should be examined. A lens that once felt comfortable may become difficult to tolerate if the surface or shape changes. Do not force a lens that causes pain. Tell the clinician about work, screen use, driving and other activities so that a proposed correction can be judged against real visual needs.

When keratoconus is suspected, ask whether the measurements show a stable shape or evidence of progression, how they compare with earlier results, and when they should be repeated. Ask what cross-linking is intended to do and whether it is suitable for the condition and stage in your own eye. It is not a shortcut to perfect uncorrected vision. A transplant is a major option reserved for selected situations; ask what less invasive approaches remain and what limitations or ongoing lens needs may continue afterwards.

For a surface infection or ulcer, understand which medicine is being used, when improvement should be apparent and what to do if it is not helping. Follow instructions about stopping contact lenses and returning for review. Some infections require close follow-up, so do not discontinue drops because pain settles before the prescribed course ends. If a prescribed medicine causes a reaction or you cannot use it as directed, call the clinician for advice rather than substituting a different bottle.

Protective habits matter especially after an earlier injury or in a person who rubs itchy eyes. Keep safety glasses available for cutting, grinding, gardening or handling cleaning products, and supervise children around sharp objects and chemicals. At work, use protection appropriate to the material and task. If an object is stuck in the eye, shield it without pressing and seek emergency help. If chemicals splash, rinse immediately and continue seeking professional care; do not wait for pain to become severe.
