        'body' => <<<'TEXT'
Before choosing treatment, ask the clinician to describe the type and direction of the deviation, whether it is constant or intermittent, how each eye sees and whether the brain is suppressing an image. The plan may be different if the main concern is a child’s visual development, an adult’s double vision or alignment. A photograph can show appearance at one moment, but treatment decisions rely on the examination and measurements. Ask what success means for your situation and how it will be checked.

If surgery is proposed, ask which muscles are being treated, whether the procedure is intended to improve alignment, double vision or another function, and whether glasses or amblyopia treatment will continue afterwards. Discuss anaesthesia, likely recovery, possible under- or over-correction, recurrence, further treatment and all alternatives. Surgery can improve alignment but cannot promise that eyes will remain perfectly straight forever or that visual development will be complete. The surgeon should explain important risks and answer questions before asking the patient or caregiver to consent.

For a child, treatment can take time and may include repeat visits during development. Make sure the caregiver understands the glasses prescription, patch schedule, drop instructions and next appointment. If the child has trouble complying, describe exactly what happens rather than changing the schedule. Small practical changes—choosing a comfortable frame, using a timer or coordinating with school—may help, but the clinician should approve any adjustment to medical treatment.

For an adult with double vision, ask what activities are safe while evaluation continues. Avoid driving if two images make it difficult to judge distance, and use caution on stairs. Sudden symptoms should be assessed urgently, especially with headache, weakness, speech changes, drooping eyelid or loss of vision. A new turn may need investigation of the cause before cosmetic or alignment treatment is considered. Once the cause and safety issues are addressed, the clinician can explain options for comfortable function.
