Families can make eye care part of ordinary child health rather than waiting for a dramatic complaint. Follow local recommendations for age-appropriate checks and arrange an assessment sooner if a child has symptoms, a visible turn, a failed screen or a clinician’s concern. A family history or premature birth may affect the recommended timing. Ask the child’s paediatrician or eye-care professional what schedule applies locally; there is not one universal timetable for every risk profile and testing setting.

Balanced daily routines support general health. Encourage outdoor play, adequate sleep, comfortable lighting and breaks during long periods of near work. These habits are not a cure for refractive error and should not be presented as a way to reverse amblyopia or strabismus. Avoid shaming a child for screen use or reading. If a child has headaches, eye strain or difficulty focusing, the answer is not automatically to remove all screens; first determine whether a vision problem, health concern or another issue needs attention.

Choose toys and activities appropriate to the child’s age, and supervise objects that can injure the eye. Use protective eyewear for sports or activities with a risk of impact or flying particles, following the sport’s safety guidance. Keep medicines, cosmetics and household chemicals out of reach. If an injury occurs, do not press on the eye or try to remove an embedded object. Seek urgent care for serious trauma, a chemical splash, sudden sight change or severe pain.

Teach older children to report changes, protect their spectacles and follow safe contact-lens hygiene if lenses are prescribed. Teenagers should understand not to share lenses, sleep or swim in them contrary to medical instructions, or use unprescribed cosmetic lenses. The prescribing clinician should decide when a child is ready to manage lenses and what adult supervision remains necessary. Positive routines and open conversations make it more likely that a child will speak up when something feels wrong rather than hiding a problem.
